# {{lb|en|graphical user interface}} The basic [[structural]] [[element]]s used in a [[graphical user interface]], such as window frames and [[scroll bar]]s, as opposed to the content.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Richard Wagner|title=Adobe AIR For Dummies|year=2009|publisher=John Wiley & Sons|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=sMHekSGEl6MC&pg=PT106&dq=%22chrome%22+UI&hl=&cd=2&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22chrome%22%20UI&f=false|isbn=978-0-470-46519-6|passage=So, just as a car might have shiny chrome trimming, a bumper, and a grill that cover the vehicle, so the '''chrome''' of an OS window surrounds and complements the window itself. Examples of '''chrome''' include the title bar, the Minimize/Maximize/Restore buttons, menu bars, and toolbars.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Jakob Nielsen|date=2012-01-29|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130307081852/https://www.nngroup.com/articles/browser-and-gui-chrome/|archivedate=2013-03-07|url=https://www.nngroup.com/articles/browser-and-gui-chrome/|title=Browser and GUI Chrome|work=Nielsen Norman Group homepage|publisher=Nielsen Norman Group|accessdate=2019-09-24|passage=Similarly [to the chrome exterior of an automobile], in most modern GUIs, the '''chrome''' lives around the edges of the screen, surrounding the middle area, which is dedicated to the user's data.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2020-01-04|quotee=Maf Vosburgh|author=Jimmy Grewal|title=Internet Explorer 5 for Mac: twentieth anniversary|work=Jimmy's Blog|url=http://jimmy.grew.al/macie5-twentieth-anniversary/|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20200109021926/http://jimmy.grew.al/macie5-twentieth-anniversary/|archivedate=2020-01-09|passage=I had the idea of making our browser '''chrome''' match the actual hardware you were on. If your Mac’s bezel was Bondi blue, we’d make our UI Bondi blue.}}
